hadiths[45]{hadithnumber,text}:
"190","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to estimate the time and gather to perform the prayer, and no one would make a call or anything similar. One day, the Muslims consulted each other about informing for prayer, whether they should ring a bell like the Christians or blow a horn like the Jews. Then Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) suggested that a man should be appointed to inform the people for prayer. Upon this, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""O Bilal! Stand up and announce the prayer."
"191","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him the Adhan in this way: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. (In the other narrations, Allahu Akbar is said four times.) Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah, Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah. Ashhadu anna Muhammadur Rasulullah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadur Rasulullah. Then he should repeat: Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah, Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah. Ashhadu anna Muhammadur Rasulullah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadur Rasulullah. Hayya ‘ala-s-Salah, twice. Hayya ‘ala-l-Falah, twice. Ishaq states that after this, he should say: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. La ilaha illallah."
"192","'Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) was instructed to say the words of the adhan twice each, and the words of the iqamah once each. And Yahya added from Ibn 'Ulayyah that I asked Ayub about this, and he said that in the iqamah only the words ""Qad qamatis salah"" should be said twice.'"
"193","Narrated Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two muezzins. One was Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) and the other was Abdullah bin Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him), who was blind."
"194","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that Sayyiduna Ibn Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him) used to give the call to prayer (adhan) for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he was blind."
"195","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would attack the enemy early in the morning and would listen attentively for the sound of the adhan. If he heard the sound of the adhan (from the opponents’ city), he would not attack them. Once, he heard a man saying ""Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar,"" so he said, ""He is a Muslim."" After that, he heard him say, ""Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah, ashhadu an la ilaha illallah,"" so he said, ""O man, you have been saved from the Fire."" When people looked, he was a shepherd of goats."
"196","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: When the call to prayer (adhan) is given, Satan turns his back and flees while passing wind so that he does not hear the adhan. After the adhan, he returns, and when the iqamah (second call to prayer) is pronounced, he again flees. After the iqamah, he comes back and puts whispers into a person's heart and reminds him of things which he had not even thought of before the prayer, so much so that the person does not remember how many rak‘ahs he has prayed."
"197","‘Isa bin Talhah says that I was with Sayyiduna Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) when the muezzin came to call him for prayer. Upon this, Sayyiduna Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) said that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say that on the Day of Resurrection, the necks of the muezzins will be the longest."
"198","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr bin al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear the call of the muezzin, say what the muezzin says, then send blessings upon me, for whoever sends blessings upon me, Allah will send ten mercies upon him. After that, ask Allah to grant me al-Wasilah, for al-Wasilah is a place in Paradise which will be given to only one of Allah’s servants, and I hope that I will be that servant. And whoever asks Allah for al-Wasilah (the praised station, i.e., a place in Paradise) for me, my intercession will become obligatory for him."
"199","Sayyiduna Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: When the muezzin says ""Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar,"" the listener should also repeat these words, and when he says ""Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah"" and ""Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah,"" the listener should also repeat these words. And when the muezzin says ""Hayya 'ala-s-Salah,"" the listener should say ""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah,"" then when the muezzin says ""Hayya 'ala-l-Falah,"" the listener should also say ""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah."" After this, when the muezzin says ""Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar"" and ""La ilaha illallah,"" the listener should also repeat these words. And when the listener says this with sincerity and firm belief, he will enter Paradise (provided that he also adheres to the pillars of Islam)."
"200","The conqueror of Persia, Sayyiduna Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: Whoever, upon hearing the call of the muezzin, says: I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, He is One and has no partner, and Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) is Allah's servant and Messenger. I am pleased and content with Allah's Lordship, with Muhammad's (peace and blessings be upon him) Messengership, and I have accepted the religion of Islam—then the sins of such a person are forgiven."
"201","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We were forbidden from asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) too many questions, so we used to like it when an intelligent person from among the desert dwellers would come and ask him, and we would listen. So, one of the desert dwellers came and said, ""O Muhammad (peace be upon him)! Your messenger came to us and said that you claim Allah has sent you?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""The messenger has spoken the truth."" The man said, ""Who created the sky?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Allah."" He said, ""Who created the earth?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Allah."" Then he said, ""Who raised the mountains and created what is in them?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Allah."" Then the man said, ""By the One who created the sky and made the earth and raised the mountains, has Allah truly sent you?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Yes."" Then the man said, ""Your messenger said that five prayers are obligatory upon us in every day and night."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""He has spoken the truth."" The man said, ""By the One who sent you, has Allah commanded you with these prayers?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Yes."" Then the man said, ""Your messenger said that there is zakat due on our wealth."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""He has spoken the truth."" The man said, ""By the One who sent you, has Allah commanded you with zakat?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Yes."" Then the man said, ""Your messenger said that fasting in Ramadan is obligatory upon us once a year."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""He has spoken the truth."" The man said, ""By the One who sent you, has Allah commanded you with these fasts?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Yes."" Then the man said, ""Your messenger said that whoever among us is able to find a way (i.e., has the means and transport and the way is safe) then Hajj to the House of Allah is obligatory upon him."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""He has spoken the truth."" Upon hearing this, the man turned away and said, ""By the One who sent you as a true Prophet, I will do no more and no less than this."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said, ""If he is truthful, he will enter Paradise."
"202","It is narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that both in residence and on a journey, two rak'ahs were prescribed as obligatory, then the prayer during travel remained as it was, but the prayer during residence was increased. Zuhri (the narrator) said: I asked 'Urwah (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Why did Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) perform the full prayer during travel? (That is, according to her, only two rak'ahs were obligatory.)"" He replied: Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) interpreted it in the same way as Sayyiduna 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) did (i.e., shortening the prayer during travel is a concession, and performing the full prayer is permissible)."
"203","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: The five prayers and from one Friday to the next Friday are expiation for the sins committed in between them, as long as major sins are not committed. And in another narration: Ramadan to the next Ramadan is expiation for the sins committed in between them, as long as major sins are not committed."
"204","Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that (for a believer) the difference between a man and disbelief and polytheism is abandoning the prayer."
"205","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr bin al-As (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The time for Zuhr begins when the sun declines and lasts until a man's shadow is equal to his height, as long as the time for Asr does not come. The time for Asr remains until the sun becomes yellow. The time for Maghrib lasts until the twilight disappears. The time for Isha lasts until the middle of the night. The time for Fajr prayer is from the appearance of dawn until the sun rises. Then, when the sun rises, refrain from prayer, for it rises between the two horns of Satan."
"206","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) that a questioner came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked about the times of prayer. The Prophet (peace be upon him) did not reply at that moment (because he intended to demonstrate by action). Then (at the time of Fajr) he ordered Bilal to give the (iqamah), and performed the Fajr prayer as soon as dawn broke, and the people could not recognize each other (due to the darkness). Then he ordered and performed the Zuhr prayer when the sun had declined, and someone would say that half the day had passed (meaning it was still midday), and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) knew best. Then he ordered and performed the Asr prayer while the sun was still high. Then he ordered and performed the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set. Then he ordered and performed the Isha prayer when the twilight had disappeared. Then on the second day, he ordered the Fajr prayer, and when he finished, someone would say that the sun had risen, or was about to rise. Then he delayed Zuhr until it was close to the time of yesterday's Asr. Then he delayed Asr until, when he finished, someone would say that the sun had turned red. Then he delayed Maghrib until it was close to the disappearance of the twilight. Then he delayed Isha until the first third of the night had passed. Then morning came, and he called the questioner and said: The time for prayer is between these two times."
"207","Muhammad bin Amr says that when Hajjaj came to Madinah, we asked Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) about the timings of the prayers. He said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to offer the Zuhr prayer at midday and in intense heat (i.e., after the sun had passed its zenith), and he used to offer the Asr prayer at a time when the sun was still bright. He would offer the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set, and then he would pray. As for the Isha prayer, sometimes he would delay it and sometimes he would offer it at the beginning of its time. When he saw that the people had gathered, he would offer it at the beginning of its time, and when he saw that the people were late in coming, he would delay it. And he used to offer the Fajr prayer in darkness."
"208","'Abu Bakr bin 'Ammarah bin Ruwaybah narrates from his father Sayyiduna 'Ammarah (may Allah be pleased with him),' he said that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saying: That person will never enter Hell who performed the prayer before sunrise and before sunset, that is, Fajr and 'Asr prayers. A man from among the people of Basrah said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: I also bear witness that I too heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). My ears heard it and my heart remembered it. (It is obvious that whoever performs these difficult prayers will surely perform the rest of the prayers as well.)"
"209","`Sayyiduna Abu Bakr bin Abu Musa Ash'ari narrates from his father, Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him), that` the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Whoever performs the two cool prayers will enter Paradise."
"210","‘Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) never left the two rak’ahs after Asr. The narrator says that Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Especially do not make it a habit to perform your prayers at the time of sunrise and sunset, that you always perform them at that time. (That is, do not delay Fajr and Asr, but rather perform them at their earliest time.)"
"211","Sayyiduna Khabbab (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and complained to him about the (scorching) intense heat, but he did not accept (our request). Zuhair said: I asked Abu Ishaq, ""Was the complaint about the Zuhr prayer?"" He said, ""Yes."" I said, ""Was it about performing the prayer at its earliest time?"" He said, ""Yes."" (That is, they requested some delay, which at first the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not accept, but later he permitted it, as is mentioned in the next hadith.)"
"212","Sayyiduna Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that when the Mu’adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) intended to give the adhan for Zuhr, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Let it cool down a bit, let it cool down a bit,” or he said: “Wait a little, wait a little.” And he said: “The severity of the heat is from the steam of Hell.” Then, when the heat is intense, perform the prayer at a cooler time. Sayyiduna Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We waited until we saw the shadows of the mounds."
"213","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to perform the Asr prayer when the sun was still high and there was heat in it. And a person would go to the high outskirts and reach there, and the sun would still be high."
"214","‘Ala bin Abdur Rahman narrates that he went to the house of Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) after offering Zuhr prayer at the house of the people of Basra, and the house of Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) was near the mosque. Then when we went to him, he asked, “Have you offered Asr?” We said, “We have just come after offering Zuhr.” He said, “Offer Asr.” So we offered Asr. When we had offered Asr, he said, “I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ‘This is the prayer of the hypocrite, that he sits watching the sun, and when it is between the two horns of Satan, he stands and pecks out four pecks, and he does not remember Allah in it except a little.’”"
"215","Sayyiduna Abu Basrah Ghifari (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in the ‘Asr prayer at the place of Makhmas and said: “This prayer was presented to those before you, but they neglected it. So whoever safeguards it will have double the reward. And after it, there is no prayer until the ‘shahid’ appears.” And by ‘shahid’ is meant a star (i.e., when a star appears)."
"216","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Whoever misses the Asr prayer, it is as if his family and wealth have been destroyed."
"217","´Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said that` the polytheists prevented the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the Asr prayer, until the sun became red or yellow. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They have prevented us from the Asr prayer, the middle prayer. May Allah fill their bellies and graves with fire. (Or he said: May Allah fill their insides and graves with fire.)"
"218","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade (performing voluntary) prayers after the Asr prayer until the sun sets, and after the Fajr prayer until the sun rises."
"219","‘Ali bin Rabah says: I heard Sayyiduna ‘Uqbah bin ‘Amir al-Juhani (may Allah be pleased with him) saying that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to forbid us from praying and from burying the dead at three times: first, when the sun is rising until it becomes high; second, when it is exactly midday until it declines; and third, when the sun is setting until it has completely set."
"220","Abu Salamah asked Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) about the two rak’ahs which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform after ‘Asr. She said that he (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform them before ‘Asr, then once he was occupied with something or forgot, so he performed them after ‘Asr, and then continued to perform them regularly. And it was the habit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that whenever he performed a prayer, he would always perform it regularly. Isma’il bin Ja’far said that what she meant was that he (peace and blessings be upon him) persisted in it."
"221","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sayyiduna Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came on the day of the Battle of Khandaq and began to speak ill of the disbelievers of Quraysh and said, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! By Allah, I was not able to perform the Asr prayer until the sun was about to set."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""By Allah, I too have not performed it."" Then we went towards a stony ground, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and we all performed ablution, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed the Asr prayer after sunset, and then after that he performed the Maghrib prayer."
"222","Mukhtar bin Fulful says: I asked Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) about offering voluntary prayers after ‘Asr. He said that Sayyiduna ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to strike the hands of those who prayed after ‘Asr, and during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), we used to offer two rak‘ahs after sunset before the Maghrib prayer. I asked him, “Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) also offer these two rak‘ahs?” He said: We used to see him praying them, but he neither commanded us to do so (i.e., as an obligation), nor did he forbid us from it."
"223","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Salamah bin Akwa’ (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would always perform the Maghrib prayer at the time when the sun would set and disappear behind the veil."
"224","`Umm al-Mu'minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that` one night the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) delayed the 'Isha prayer until a large part of the night had passed and those who were in the mosque had fallen asleep. Then he (peace be upon him) came out, offered the prayer, and said: If I did not think that I would cause hardship for my ummah, this would be its (preferred) time."
"225","`Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates that` the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not let the Bedouins overpower you regarding the name of the Isha prayer, for in the Book of Allah it is called 'Isha.' They delay the milking of their camels, and that is why they call the Isha prayer 'Atamah.'"
"226","Sayyiduna Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: ""What will you do when there will be such rulers over you who will delay the prayer till its last time, or he said: who will destroy the prayer from its proper time?"" I said, ""What do you command me to do?"" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Perform the prayer at its (proper) time, then if you pray with them as well, it will be a voluntary prayer for you."
"227","‘Abdullah bin Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with them both) says: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), “Which deed is the best?” (i.e., which has the greatest reward). He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “To perform prayer at its proper time.” I said, “Then which?” He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “To be good to parents” (i.e., to keep them happy and pleased, to do good to them, and to treat their friends well also). I said, “Then which?” He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “To strive in the way of Allah.” Then, out of consideration for him (peace and blessings be upon him), I stopped asking further (so that it would not be burdensome for him)."
"228","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: Whoever catches one rak'ah of a prayer has caught that prayer."
"229","Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) addressed us and said: ""Today, after the sun passes its zenith, and throughout the night, keep moving; if Allah wills, you will reach water by morning."" So the people set out in such a way that no one paid attention to anyone else. Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) kept moving until half the night had passed, and I was at his side. He (peace and blessings be upon him) began to doze off and leaned over on his mount (i.e., overcome by sleep), and I came and supported him (so that he would not fall), without waking him, until he (peace and blessings be upon him) sat upright again. Then we continued until much of the night had passed, and again he (peace and blessings be upon him) leaned over, and I again supported him without waking him, until he (peace and blessings be upon him) sat upright again. Then we continued until the last part of the night, and then he (peace and blessings be upon him) leaned over much more than the previous two times, almost about to fall. Then I came and supported him. Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) raised his head and said, ""Who is this?"" I replied, ""Abu Qatadah."" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""How long have you been walking with me like this?"" I replied, ""I have been walking with you like this since the night began."" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""May Allah protect you as you have protected His Prophet."" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Do you see that we are hidden from the people's sight?"" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Do you see anyone?"" I said, ""Here is a rider,"" then I said, ""Here is another rider,"" until we gathered seven riders. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) moved aside from the path and laid his head on the ground (i.e., to sleep) and said, ""You people, take care of our prayer (i.e., wake us at the time of prayer)."" The first to wake up was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) himself, and the sun was on his back. Then we all woke up in a panic, and he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Mount up!"" So we mounted and continued until the sun had risen high. Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) dismounted and asked for his ablution vessel, which was with me, and there was a little water in it. Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) performed ablution with it (less than the usual ablution, i.e., with very little water and very quickly), and a little water remained in it. Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said to Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Put our vessel aside, for it will have a strange quality."" Then Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) called the adhan for prayer, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed two rak‘ahs, then performed the obligatory morning prayer, and performed it as he did every day. Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) and we mounted with him, and some of us whispered to others, ""What will be the expiation for our shortcoming in the prayer today?"" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Am I not an example for you?"" Then he said, ""What fault is there in sleep? The fault is when a man does not pray until the time for the next prayer comes (i.e., he deliberately delays it until it is missed). So whoever does so (i.e., misses it and makes it up), it is obligatory that when he becomes aware, he should perform it. And when the next day comes, he should perform his prayer at its appointed time (i.e., just because it was missed once does not mean the time for prayer changes)."" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""What do you think the people have done?"" Then he said, ""When the people woke up in the morning and did not find their Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said, 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) is behind you; he would not leave you behind.' And other people said, 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) is ahead of you.' If the people had listened to Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), they would have found the right path (this information was given by the Prophet as a miracle)."" The narrator said: Then we reached the people when the day had advanced and everything had become hot, and the people began to say, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! We are dying and thirsty."" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""No, you are not dead."" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Bring our small bowl,"" and he asked for the vessel, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) began to pour water, and Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) began to give people water to drink. When the people saw that there was only one vessel full of water, they rushed towards it (i.e., each person feared that the water was little and he might miss out). Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Take it calmly and gently; you will all be satisfied."" So the people began to take it calmly, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) kept pouring water, and I kept giving it to drink, until none remained except me and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The narrator said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) poured again and said to me, ""Drink!"" I said, ""I will not drink until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) drinks."" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""The one who serves the people drinks last."" So I drank. The narrator said: Then the people happily and contentedly reached the water. The narrator said: Abdullah bin Rabah said: I used to narrate this hadith to people in the congregational mosque, when Sayyiduna Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""O young man, consider what you are saying, for I was one of the riders that night."" He said: I said, ""Then you must be well acquainted with this hadith."" He said, ""Which tribe are you from?"" I said, ""I am from the Ansar."" He said, ""Narrate, for you people know your hadiths well."" Then I narrated the whole story to the people. Then Sayyiduna Imran (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""I was also present that night, but I do not know that anyone remembered it as well as you have."
"230","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""Is it permissible to pray in a single garment?"" So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""Does each one of you have two garments?"
"231","Sayyiduna Umar bin Abi Salamah (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) praying in the house of Umm al-Mu'mineen Umm Salamah, wrapped in a cloth, and both its ends were on his shoulders."
"232","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood to pray wearing a cloak with patterns on it, and he began to look at its designs. When he finished the prayer, he said: Take this cloak to Abu Juham bin Hudhayfah and bring me his cloak (which was without patterns), because this cloak distracted me just now in my prayer."
"233","Ishaq bin Abdullah bin Abu Talha narrates from our master Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that his (Anas's) grandmother, whose name was Malikah, invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to a meal which she had prepared. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ate and said: Stand up so that I may pray (for your goodness and blessings). Our master Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said that I stood up with a (mat) made of palm fibers which had become black due to much use (i.e., it was worn out), I sprinkled water on it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood on it. I and an orphan formed a row behind him, and the old woman stood behind us. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in two rak‘ahs of prayer and concluded with salam."
"234","Saeed bin Yazid says: I asked Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) pray while wearing shoes?"" He said, ""Yes."
